PLAIN-SPEAK QUESTION

A49 showed us
that the core can organize
the cells that are available.

The reservoir diagnosis
showed us the problem:

during the critical window,
most missing cells
are not available.

They are still on the bench.

A48 showed
that G5 can safely
get some of those cells
back into circulation sooner.

So A50 asks
the clean layered question.

First:

does recovery
put more cells back on the field?

Second:

does the challenged core
then organize those newly available cells?

Third:

does the combination
do more than simply adding
the two separate improvements together?

Nothing gets stronger.

Nothing gets tuned.

We are only connecting
two mechanisms
that already work
on two different bottlenecks.

The test is:

DOES RECOVERY
SUPPLY THE CELLS

THAT SPATIAL ORGANIZATION
WAS MISSING?

And if it does,

DOES THE WHOLE ORGANISM
FINALLY PRESERVE
A STABLE MAJORITY
THROUGH THE HARD CHALLENGE?

